{"key":"u13-apphycem-mcq-029-v1","kind":"mcq","model":"google/gemini-3.8-flash","pass":"solve","ok":true,"object":{"chosen_label":"D","final_answer":"A component along the surface would push charges along it, so equilibrium allows none","working":"In electrostatic equilibrium, free charges in a conductor are at rest. If there were a non-zero component of the electric field tangent to the surface of the conductor (just outside or at the surface), free electrons along the surface would experience an electric force F = qE_parallel and accelerate, creating a surface current. Because charges are stationary in equilibrium, no net tangential force can act on them. Hence, the tangential component of the electric field must be zero, meaning the electric field just outside the surface must be purely normal (perpendicular) to the surface.","defect":""},"usage":{"inputTokens":403,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":403,"cacheReadTokens":0},"outputTokens":391,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":162,"reasoningTokens":229},"totalTokens":794,"raw":{"thoughtsTokenCount":229,"promptTokenCount":403,"candidatesTokenCount":162,"totalTokenCount":794,"trafficType":"ON_DEMAND","promptTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":403}],"candidatesTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":162}]},"reasoningTokens":229,"cachedInputTokens":0},"attempts":1,"mode":"object","ms":3530,"keyed_label":"D"}
